---
import "@/styles/article.css";

import SectionHeader from "@/components/SectionHeader.astro";
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import { PROFILE_NAME } from "@/lib/site";

const title = `A Personal Trust Control Plane | ${PROFILE_NAME}`;
const description =
  "Design notes on making a public identity verifiable, monitorable, and recoverable — without depending on any single platform.";
---

<Base description={description} path="/trust-control-plane" title={title}>
  <main>
    <section>
      <SectionHeader
        label="Article"
        no="2026-07"
        title="A Personal Trust Control Plane"
      />

      <p class="lead">
        How I made my public identity verifiable, monitorable, and recoverable —
        without depending on any single platform. A clearsigned copy of this
        article is at{" "}
        <a href="/trust-control-plane.txt.asc">/trust-control-plane.txt.asc</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">The problem</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Most of what proves "who I am" as an engineer lives inside platforms: a
        verified badge here, an OAuth login there, a blue checkmark somewhere
        else. Each of these works until the platform changes its policy,
        suspends the account, gets acquired, or shuts down. None of them
        compose: my GitHub account says nothing cryptographically about my
        domain, my domain says nothing about my Ethereum address, and if I lost
        a key tomorrow, none of them would tell you what to trust instead.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        I wanted something that answers four questions, verifiably and durably:
      </p>

      <ol class="numberList">
        <li>Who currently controls each public identifier?</li>
        <li>
          What is the current canonical state, and how can a third party verify
          it?
        </li>
        <li>What changed over time, and how can earlier states be audited?</li>
        <li>
          If a key, device, or account is compromised — or I am unavailable —
          how do revocation and migration happen?
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="bodyText">
        This article describes the system I built to answer them: a
        <em>personal trust control plane</em>. Not a product, not a protocol
        proposal — a working setup for one person, with the design decisions and
        the mistakes made along the way.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Control plane vs. distribution plane</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The first structural decision: separate where identity is
        <em>managed</em> from where it is <em>served</em>.
      </p>

      <ul class="itemList">
        <li>
          A private control-plane repository holds the expected identity
          definition, signing targets, pre-publication artifacts, verification
          code, runbooks, and an audit trail of every change. It contains no
          secrets — no seeds, no private keys, no passphrases. It manages policy
          and procedure, not key material.
        </li>
        <li>
          A public distribution plane (this website and its public repository)
          serves what third parties actually fetch: keys, signed manifests,
          proofs, and a verifier.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="bodyText">
        This separation means the public site can be rebuilt from scratch after
        a compromise, because the source of truth for "what should be published"
        never lived there.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Keys: an offline root and machine-scoped subkeys</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">The OpenPGP layout is deliberately boring:</p>

      <ul class="itemList">
        <li>
          An Ed25519 certify-only primary key that stays offline and is used
          only to manage subkeys and identities.
        </li>
        <li>
          Machine-scoped signing subkeys: each machine holds its own signing
          subkey's private material, and no private subkey is shared across
          machines. A compromised laptop means revoking that laptop's subkeys,
          not rebuilding my identity.
        </li>
        <li>Separate encryption and SSH-authentication subkeys.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="bodyText">
        My previous RSA-4096 key (2020–2026) was revoked as
        <em>superseded</em>, not silently abandoned — the revocation is
        published through the same channels as the key itself, so verifiers see
        an explicit transition rather than a dangling old key. The full layout
        is on the <a href="/keys">key policy page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">
        Machine-readable identity, signed and history-linked
      </h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Human-readable "about" pages rot. The canonical state is instead a
        machine-readable manifest at a well-known URL:
      </p>

      <ul class="itemList">
        <li>
          <a href="/.well-known/identity.json"
            ><code class="inlineCode">/.well-known/identity.json</code></a
          >{" "}— every current identifier (OpenPGP fingerprint, Ethereum
          address, ENS name, DNS domains, Nostr, Bluesky, GitHub), plus expiry
          dates, verifier hash, and history references.
        </li>
        <li>A detached OpenPGP signature over the exact manifest bytes.</li>
        <li>
          An append-only history: every change to the manifest is an event, and
          old states are published as content-addressed checkpoints, so
          rewriting the past is detectable.
        </li>
        <li>
          A pinned public verifier: a dependency-free script whose SHA-256 is
          embedded in the signed manifest. Anyone can run it and check every
          claim — no API keys, no secrets, no trust in my CI.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="heading">The trust bootstrap problem</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        There is a circularity trap in self-hosted identity: if you fetch my
        key, my manifest, <em>and</em> my verifier all from the same website, then
        an attacker who fully controls the site can hand you a consistent set of lies.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The bootstrap procedure therefore never treats the site alone as a trust
        root. A first-time verifier is told to compare the fingerprint across
        channels with different failure domains — DNS TXT records on two
        separate domains, WKD on a third domain, the OpenPGP signature on the
        public repository's main branch — and ideally against an out-of-band
        copy (a business card, a previously known signature). The verification
        instructions explicitly record <em>which channel you started from</em>,
        because the guarantee you get depends on it. Honest limits are part of
        the design: the procedure documents what it does <em>not</em> prove (legal
        identity, global uniqueness, device security), not just what it does.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Bridging OpenPGP and Ethereum</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Neither PGP nor Ethereum depends on a platform, so they make a good pair
        — but they know nothing about each other. The bridge is a pair of signed
        statements: the OpenPGP key signs a statement naming the Ethereum
        address, and the Ethereum key signs a statement naming the OpenPGP
        fingerprint. Both are <a href="/proofs">published</a> and re-verified continuously,
        including signature recovery back to the exact address. Either signature alone
        would be spoofable by someone who controls just one side; the pair binds them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">ENS is an adapter, not a foundation</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        This was the decision I got wrong first. The initial plan treated ENS
        registration as a prerequisite. It is not: ENS is a name-resolution
        adapter on top of an Ethereum address that must already be verifiable
        without it. I re-planned around the principle that an ENS or IPFS outage
        must never block signing, recovery, or attestation, then registered <code
          class="inlineCode">yhay81.eth</code
        > later, as an add-on.
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The ENS profile itself is two-layered: the contenthash points to an
        immutable IPFS capsule (a snapshot that can never silently change),
        while the capsule points back to the canonical HTTPS site for
        <em>current</em> revocation state and complete proofs. Immutability where
        it helps, freshness where it matters.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">
        Monitoring: checks that state their exact guarantee
      </h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        A weekly healthcheck verifies the whole surface using public information
        only: manifest signature, history integrity, verifier hash,
        cross-attestations, WKD, DNS proofs, Nostr, Bluesky, GitHub commit
        signatures, ENS records, and OpenTimestamps artifacts. Two design rules
        turned out to matter more than the check count:
      </p>

      <ol class="numberList">
        <li>
          Every check states its exact guarantee. The OpenTimestamps check, for
          example, reports "timestamp artifacts present" separately from full
          cryptographic verification, instead of a vague green checkmark. A
          check that overstates its guarantee is worse than no check.
        </li>
        <li>
          Expiry becomes "degraded" 30 days early. Keys, manifests,
          security.txt, and ENS registration all expire; the system nags me
          before third parties see anything broken.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The healthcheck runs in CI — but CI is itself a platform dependency, so
        an independent watchdog runs on my own machine outside GitHub Actions,
        and verifies the hash of its own installed verifier against the signed
        manifest (a stale or tampered monitor is itself a failure mode).
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Recovery is a feature, not an afterthought</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The system assumes keys leak, devices vanish, and people become
        unavailable. Prepared responses include:
      </p>

      <ul class="itemList">
        <li>
          Scenario-based runbooks: device compromise, subkey vs. master-key
          leak, Ethereum seed leak (an address cannot be revoked — only migrated
          from), account takeovers, and death/disaster handover.
        </li>
        <li>
          An offline recovery kit: signed git bundles of both repositories,
          runbooks, and public evidence, with a checksum manifest and a detached
          OpenPGP signature. The generator refuses to run on a dirty worktree,
          so a kit always corresponds to a signed, published state.
        </li>
        <li>
          Envelope separation for physical handover: secrets in one sealed
          envelope, procedures and contacts in another, so a successor can
          locate and understand before opening anything sensitive.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="heading">A maturity model instead of checkboxes</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The single most useful process decision: no capability is "done". Each
        one is tracked through five states —
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        <code class="inlineCode"
          >Designed → Implemented → Published → Monitored → Rehearsed</code
        >
      </p>

      <p class="bodyText">
        — and the honest answer is that some of mine currently sit at
        "implemented but not yet rehearsed", and the status table says so. The
        same honesty applies to attestations: the framework for third-party
        attestations exists and is continuously verified, and the health report
        states plainly that zero real attestations exist yet. A trust system
        that rounds "framework exists" up to "verified" would be lying about
        exactly the thing it is supposed to guarantee.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">Delegating operations to an AI, explicitly</h2>

      <p class="bodyText">
        Much of this infrastructure is operated together with an AI coding
        agent. That could easily undermine the whole point ("who controls the
        key?"), so the delegation boundary is explicit and published in the
        <a href="/keys">key policy</a>:
      </p>

      <ul class="itemList">
        <li>
          After I review and approve exact content, the agent may run signing
          commands using machine-scoped subkeys, regenerate manifests, and run
          verification.
        </li>
        <li>
          Only I perform master-key ceremonies, authorize production publishing,
          send on-chain transactions, and handle secrets and physical media.
        </li>
        <li>
          The agent never sees private key material or passphrases — signing
          goes through the local key agent.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every commit is OpenPGP-signed, so the git history is also an audit
          trail of what the agent executed.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="bodyText">
        In 2026, "who controls this identity" increasingly includes "what is
        delegated to machines". Writing the boundary down — and publishing it —
        seems more honest than pretending the delegation does not exist.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading">What I would tell someone starting this</h2>

      <ol class="numberList">
        <li>
          Start from the four questions, not from tools. ENS, Keyoxide, and
          badges are adapters; control, verifiability, history, and recovery are
          the actual requirements.
        </li>
        <li>
          Track "implemented", "published", "monitored", and "rehearsed" as
          different states. The gap between them is where trust systems quietly
          fail.
        </li>
        <li>
          Make every check state its exact guarantee — including what it does
          not prove.
        </li>
        <li>
          Plan the exit first. Revocation, migration, and handover are the parts
          you cannot retrofit after the incident.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep the non-goals visible. No badge collecting, no single trust
          score, no premature generalization into a framework nobody asked for.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="bodyText">
        The verification procedure, trust-bootstrap instructions, and public
        verifier are linked from the <a href="/identity">identity overview</a>.
        This article is signed with the key it describes:{" "}
        <a href="/trust-control-plane.txt.asc">clearsigned source</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="updated">Published: 2026-07-21 · Signed with subkey 828C52C5</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
